Part of #30 

~~Very much a work in progress but it seems to be relatively well. For details about how well it currently performs see below.~~

To start, a disclaimer that this is well out of my comfort zone and I've completely made this stuff up as I go along. It's totally possible that I'm making incorrect statements or that something exists which does this in a much better way.

I think this is about ready enough now. In summary it takes a compiled x86_64 ELF file (i.e. Linux) and runs a disassembler to get the corresponding assembly code. It then crudely parses the `PyInit_<name>` function to figure out what methods and objects are going to be available if it's loaded. This will never be perfect as it's completely possibly for binaries to generate this information with loops and string manipulation which would require full emulation (perhaps possible with `unicorn` though remains to be seen if it's worth it). For now I handle calls to functions and some basic register/stack manipulation but more complex instructions and looping structures are ignored.

## Tasks for future PRs

- [ ] Change the returned object of `CompiledPythonLib:find_symbols` to be consistent with whatever the final data formal should be
- [ ] Related to above, extract more information. Currently only the names are know but basic types are quite easy to extract
- [ ] Slowly support more patterns and functions in the parsing of the assembly
- [ ] Fail more gracefully in the hope of getting partial results
- [ ] Add some limits to prevent getting stuck in infinite loops (rare but there are two skipped tests which trigger this)
- [ ] Stop using `pwntools` and just run `objdump` directly
- [ ] Support macOS and maybe windows binaries (naively this shouldn't be too hard as I assume disassembly tools must also exist)
- [ ] Support other architectures like aarch64 and ppcle64 (tricker, lends itself towards using something like `unicorn`)

## Example usage:

```bash
$ python -m symbol_exporter.python_so_extractor "$(python -c 'import _ssl; print(_ssl.__file__)')" --print --compare _ssl
```

## Details about how it works

Using [ghidra](https://ghidra-sre.org/) you can decompile Python 3.7's `math.cpython-37m-x86_64-linux-gnu.so`. The automated analysis of the binary is really nice though it's also quite a heavy dependency and relatively slow so I haven't looked into using it directly for this PR. It's just a nice way to get a better view of what's going on.

 Looking at the annotated assembly (left) and decompiled C (right) corrosponding to `PyInit_math`:

<img width="1246" alt="Screenshot 2021-02-23 at 11 56 15" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5220533/108834210-2f2b1f00-75ce-11eb-85b9-9a15c6de3b25.png">

The first interesting thing is the call to `PyModule_Create2` with the `mathmodule` struct. Looking at the address loaded with `LEA RDI,xxx` you can see the pointers to the module name, doc string and methods:

<img width="1098" alt="Screenshot 2021-02-23 at 12 01 22" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5220533/108834989-2129ce00-75cf-11eb-9062-615d71f3309d.png">

The methods pointer is an array of sturcts with the name and docstring:

<img width="1213" alt="Screenshot 2021-02-23 at 12 05 01" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5220533/108835237-7d8ced80-75cf-11eb-8882-a81421120813.png">

This is a nice easy case however it can get much messier. For example `_codecs_jp` is decompiled to the below code (note the `do...while` loop calling `PyModule_AddObject`). I think it's probably best to start running the code and see if there are any widely used patterns which need to be supported.

```c
long PyInit__codecs_jp(void)

{
  int iVar1;
  long lVar2;
  undefined8 uVar3;
  long lVar4;
  dbcs_map *pdVar5;
  char *__src;
  undefined4 *puVar6;
  long in_FS_OFFSET;
  byte bVar7;
  undefined8 local_138;
  undefined8 local_130;
  undefined4 local_128 [62];
  long local_30;
  
  bVar7 = 0;
  local_30 = *(long *)(in_FS_OFFSET + 0x28);
  lVar2 = PyModule_Create2(&__module,0x3f5);
  if (lVar2 != 0) {
    __src = "jisx0208";
    pdVar5 = _mapping_list;
    do {
      lVar4 = 0x3c;
      local_138 = 0x5f70616d5f5f;
      puVar6 = local_128;
      while (lVar4 != 0) {
        lVar4 = lVar4 + -1;
        *puVar6 = 0;
        puVar6 = puVar6 + (ulong)bVar7 * -2 + 1;
      }
      local_130 = 0;
      strcpy((char *)((long)&local_138 + 6),__src);
      uVar3 = PyCapsule_New(pdVar5,"multibytecodec.__map_*",0);
      iVar1 = PyModule_AddObject(lVar2,&local_138,uVar3);
      if (iVar1 == -1) break;
      pdVar5 = pdVar5 + 1;
      __src = pdVar5->charset;
    } while (*__src != '\0');
  }
  if (local_30 == *(long *)(in_FS_OFFSET + 0x28)) {
    return lVar2;
  }
                    /* WARNING: Subroutine does not return */
  __stack_chk_fail();
}
```

# 16166번 서울의 지하철
[문제 보러가기](https://www.acmicpc.net/problem/16166)

## 🅰 설계

처음에 트리를 사용해서 역들을 정렬해서 최단 경로를 찾으려 했습니다.

접근이 잘못된 것 같아서 엎고 규태님의 코드를 참고해서 호선을 노드로 하여 BFS를 하였습니다.

---

### 1. 선언부

```java
static int N, D, cnt = 0;
static HashMap<Integer, List<Integer>> map = new HashMap<>(); // key : 역번호, value : 연결된 호선 리스트 
static List<Integer>[] lines; // 호선별 역정보 
static boolean[] visited; // 호선 방문 여부
```
map에 역과 그 역을 지나는 호선들을 저장했습니다.

따로 호선별 역정보를 리스트배열에 저장하였고,

boolean배열로 호선들의 방문 여부를 체크했습니다.

---

### 1. main

```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws NumberFormatException, IOException {
		BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in)); 
		N = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine());
		lines = new List[N+1];
		visited = new boolean[N+1];
		StringTokenizer st;
		
		for (int l = 1; l <= N; l++) { // 호선 번호
			st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());
			int K = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken()); // 호선의 역 개수
			ArrayList<Integer> line = new ArrayList<>(); // 현 호선의 역정보
			
			for (int j = 0; j < K; j++) {
				int n = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken()); // 역 번호
				line.add(n);
				
				if(map.containsKey(n)) { // 이미 있는 역이면 호선 추가
					List<Integer> lines = map.get(n);
					lines.add(l);
					map.put(n, lines);
				}
				else  { // 없던 역이면 map에 추가
					List<Integer> lines = new ArrayList<>();
					lines.add(l);
					map.put(n, lines); 
				}
			}
			lines[l] = line;
		}
		D = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine()); // 종착역 번호
		
		bfs();
		
		System.out.println(cnt);
	}
```
1호선부터 입력받으며 map과 list에 각 정보를 입력하고 dfs를 실행했습니다.

---

### 2. bfs

```java
private static void bfs() {
		Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>(); // 방문할 호선을 저장 
		
		for(int line : map.get(0)) { 
			q.add(line); // 출발역의 호선 큐에 저장
			visited[line] = true; // 호선 방문 처리
		}
		
		while (!q.isEmpty()) {
			int size = q.size();
			
			for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) { // 현 단계의 모든 호선 탐색
				int cur = q.remove(); // 현재 호선
				
				if(lines[cur].contains(D)) return; // 도착역이 있는 호선이면 탐색 종료
				
				// 현 호선 역들의 환승 호선 찾기
				for(int num : lines[cur]) 
				for(int line : map.get(num)) {
					if(visited[line]) continue;
					q.add(line);
					visited[line] = true;
				}
			}
	    	cnt++;	// 다음 단계로 넘어감 
		}
		cnt = -1; // 도착역을 못찾은 경우
	}
```
너비 우선 탐색을 하며 출발역의 모든 호선을 탐색하고,

그 호선들과 이어지는 다른 호선들을 환승하여 다음 단계에 탐색하였습니다.

---

## ✅ 후기
입력 받은 정보를 탐색에 용이하게 전처리를 하여 맵이나 리스트에 저장하는 과정이 익숙하지 않았습니다.

트리를 사용한 집합과 맵이라는 유형의 문제들을 더 풀어보며 감을 잡아가겠습니다.

@wbthomason, this resolves the issue raised in gitter and discussed in #187. It allows specifying an `env` key for `luarocks` so you can pass arguments such as `OPENSSL_DIR` for the `openssl` rock, as an example.
```lua
 use_rocks {"openssl", env = {OPENSSL_DIR = "<value>"}}
```
@clason could you try this branch out and see if it does what you need.

